"""In-depth material for the Compiler Design lessons."""
from learn import deepen

deepen("cd-phases-lexical",
       proofs=[
           ("Maximal munch resolves token ambiguity, and a lexer is a DFA",
            """A scanner is specified by regular expressions r₁ … rₖ, one per token class, in priority order. Combine them as r₁ + … + rₖ and convert to a DFA (via an NFA with one start state and ε-moves, then the subset construction), remembering for every accepting DFA state which rᵢ of lowest index it accepts. To scan, run the DFA from the current position and remember the **last** accepting state passed and the input position there; when the DFA reaches a dead state, report the token of the last accepting state and restart from the recorded position. This returns the longest prefix matching some rule (maximal munch) and uses the rule order to break ties, so `>=` is one token and `ifx` is an identifier (the longer identifier match beats the keyword `if`). The backtracking is bounded by the longest token and the whole scan is linear for most token sets."""),
           ("Counting tokens: why whitespace and comments are not tokens",
            """The parser's grammar is written over a finite vocabulary of token classes. Whitespace and comments carry no syntactic meaning, so the lexer's rules for them have an empty action (they are consumed and discarded), and they never appear in the token stream. In `if (a1 >= 10) x = y + 2.5;` the lexemes are if, (, a1, >=, 10, ), x, =, y, +, 2.5, ; — twelve tokens; the number of **distinct token classes** (keyword, identifier, number, operators, punctuation) is smaller than the token count, and the number of distinct lexemes may be smaller still if a lexeme repeats."""),
           ("The front end/back end split and why intermediate code helps",
            """With n source languages and m target machines, direct translators need n·m compilers. A common intermediate representation (IR) needs n front ends (source → IR) and m back ends (IR → machine): n + m components. Optimisations that work on the IR are written once and benefit every combination. This is the architecture of production compilers (e.g. LLVM). The phases communicate through data structures: tokens → parse tree → annotated syntax tree with the symbol table → IR → optimised IR → target code."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Phases, passes and tools",
            """Lexical analysis (regular expressions, DFAs), syntax analysis (context-free grammars, parsers), semantic analysis (type checking with the symbol table), intermediate code generation, machine-independent optimisation, code generation and machine-dependent optimisation. The **symbol table** (hash table keyed by identifier) records names, types, scopes and storage offsets; error handling is spread across phases. A **pass** is one traversal of the whole program; a single-pass compiler needs declaration before use. Tools: Lex/Flex for scanners, Yacc/Bison for LALR(1) parsers. The preprocessor handles includes and macros; the assembler, linker and loader are not compiler phases."""),
           ("Lexical errors and recovery",
            """Typical lexical errors: illegal characters, an unterminated string or comment, an over-long identifier or number. Recovery strategies: panic mode (delete characters until a valid token starts), insert a missing character, replace or transpose adjacent characters. Syntax errors (missing semicolon, mismatched brackets) and semantic errors (type mismatch, undeclared variable) belong to later phases. Input buffering uses a pair of buffer halves with sentinels so that the scanner can look ahead without checking the end of buffer at every character."""),
       ])

deepen("cd-top-down-parsing",
       proofs=[
           ("Eliminating left recursion preserves the language",
            """The rules A → Aα₁ | … | Aαₘ | β₁ | … | βₙ generate the strings β followed by any sequence of α's: βαᵢ₁αᵢ₂…αᵢₖ. The grammar A → β₁A′ | … | βₙA′, A′ → α₁A′ | … | αₘA′ | ε generates exactly the same strings (a β followed by zero or more α's) but its derivations are right-recursive, so a top-down parser never loops. The parse tree changes shape (right-leaning instead of left-leaning), which matters for associativity of operators and is repaired by semantic actions when building the syntax tree. Left factoring A → αβ₁ | αβ₂ becomes A → αA′, A′ → β₁ | β₂, postponing the choice until the common prefix has been consumed."""),
           ("LL(1) condition: disjoint predict sets",
            """For A → α | β, define predict(A → α) = FIRST(α) ∪ (FOLLOW(A) if α ⇒* ε). The grammar is LL(1) iff for every non-terminal the predict sets of its productions are pairwise disjoint. Necessity: if a terminal a lies in two predict sets, the table entry M[A, a] contains two productions and the parser cannot decide with one token of look-ahead. Sufficiency: with disjoint predict sets the table has at most one production per cell, and the stack-based parser makes a unique choice at each step, building the leftmost derivation deterministically. Consequences: a left-recursive grammar is never LL(1) (FIRST(Aα) overlaps FIRST(β) of the same non-terminal) and an ambiguous grammar is never LL(1)."""),
           ("Computing FOLLOW by fixed-point iteration",
            """Initialise FOLLOW(S) = {$} and all others empty. Repeat until nothing changes: for each production A → αBβ, add FIRST(β) ∖ {ε} to FOLLOW(B); if β ⇒* ε (or β is empty) add FOLLOW(A) to FOLLOW(B). The sets only grow and are bounded by the finite terminal set, so the iteration terminates; it computes the least solution of the defining inequalities, which is exactly the set of terminals that can follow B in a sentential form. For E → TX, X → +TX | ε, T → FY, Y → *FY | ε, F → (E) | id: FOLLOW(E) = FOLLOW(X) = {$, )}, FOLLOW(T) = FOLLOW(Y) = {+, $, )}, FOLLOW(F) = {*, +, $, )}."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Table-driven predictive parser",
            """A stack holds grammar symbols (initially $ S) and a table M[A, a] gives the production to apply. Loop: let X be the top and a the next token. If X = a = $ accept; if X is a terminal equal to a, pop and advance; if X is a non-terminal and M[X, a] = X → Y₁…Yₖ, pop X and push Yₖ…Y₁; otherwise error. The number of non-empty table cells equals Σ over productions of |predict set|. Recursive descent implements the same decisions as mutually recursive procedures; error recovery uses synchronising sets drawn from FOLLOW. Grammar classes: LL(1) ⊂ LL(k); LL(1) grammars exclude left recursion, common prefixes and ambiguity."""),
           ("Common FIRST/FOLLOW pitfalls",
            """FIRST(ε) = {ε}; FOLLOW never contains ε; $ belongs to FOLLOW of the start symbol and of anything that can end a sentence; when a production A → αB ends with B, FOLLOW(B) ⊇ FOLLOW(A); when B is followed by a nullable string, take FIRST of the string without ε and continue with the next symbols until a non-nullable one is found. Fill the ε-production cells using FOLLOW(A), not FIRST. A grammar with two nullable alternatives for the same non-terminal is never LL(1)."""),
       ])

deepen("cd-bottom-up-parsing",
       proofs=[
           ("LR(0) automaton: closure and goto, and the state count",
            """An LR(0) item is a production with a dot marking how much has been seen. closure(I) adds B → ·γ for every item A → α·Bβ in I (the parser might start recognising B), repeating until no change. goto(I, X) takes all items A → α·Xβ in I, advances the dot to A → αX·β and closes the result. The canonical collection starts from closure({S′ → ·S}) for the augmented grammar and applies goto for each grammar symbol until no new sets appear; these item sets are the DFA states of a recogniser of viable prefixes. For E → E + T | T, T → T * F | F, F → (E) | id the construction yields 12 states, and for S → AA, A → aA | b it yields 7. The viable-prefix DFA is the reason LR parsers can decide shift/reduce from the stack contents alone."""),
           ("SLR(1) conflict analysis: S → L = R | R, L → *R | id, R → L is not SLR(1)",
            """FOLLOW(R) = {=, $}: R appears at the end of S → R and L → *R (so FOLLOW(S) = {$} and FOLLOW(L) ⊆ FOLLOW(R)), and L appears before = in S → L = R so = ∈ FOLLOW(L), and from R → L, FOLLOW(L) ⊇ FOLLOW(R); therefore = ∈ FOLLOW(R). One state contains both S → L·= R (shift on =) and R → L· (reduce on every symbol of FOLLOW(R), including =): a **shift/reduce conflict**, so the grammar is not SLR(1). A canonical LR(1) parser attaches precise lookaheads: the item R → L· in that state has lookahead $ only (= can never legitimately follow in that context), so there is no conflict and the grammar is LR(1)."""),
           ("The grammar-class hierarchy: LR(0) ⊂ SLR(1) ⊂ LALR(1) ⊂ LR(1)",
            """LR(0) reduces whenever a complete item is present (no lookahead); SLR(1) reduces only on terminals in FOLLOW(A), which is a subset of 'all terminals', so any LR(0) table is also SLR(1). LALR(1) reduces only on the lookaheads actually possible in the merged state — a subset of FOLLOW(A) — so SLR(1) ⇒ LALR(1). CLR(1) keeps states with distinct lookaheads separate and so has at most the conflicts of LALR(1). LALR merges CLR states that have the same LR(0) core; merging cannot create shift/reduce conflicts (those depend only on the core's items and lookahead sets whose union keeps the shift symbols), but can create reduce/reduce conflicts. LALR(1) has the same number of states as LR(0)/SLR(1), whereas CLR(1) can have many more."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Shift–reduce mechanics and conflict resolution",
            """A shift–reduce parser reads left to right and produces a rightmost derivation in reverse. The **handle** is the substring matching the right side of a production whose reduction leads back to the start symbol; viable prefixes are the stack contents that can occur. Conflicts: shift/reduce (resolved in YACC in favour of shift, or by precedence/associativity declarations) and reduce/reduce (resolved by the production listed first). Operator-precedence parsing works for operator grammars with no ε-productions and no two adjacent non-terminals. Every deterministic CFL has an LR(1) grammar; LL(k) ⊂ LR(k); LR parsers detect syntax errors as early as possible (the viable-prefix property)."""),
           ("Building parsing tables",
            """ACTION[state, a]: shift j if goto(state, a) = j; reduce A → α for each complete item (for SLR on FOLLOW(A), for LALR/CLR on the item's lookaheads, for LR(0) on all terminals); accept for S′ → S· on $. GOTO[state, A] = state after a non-terminal. The number of table entries is states × (terminals + non-terminals). An LR(0) grammar must not have a state mixing a complete item with other items; an SLR table with two actions in a cell shows a conflict. Count states carefully, including the state for the augmenting production."""),
       ])

deepen("cd-syntax-directed",
       proofs=[
           ("S-attributed definitions can be evaluated during bottom-up (LR) parsing",
            """In an S-attributed definition every attribute is synthesized: a node's attributes depend only on its children's. An LR parser performs a reduction A → X₁…Xₖ exactly when the children X₁…Xₖ have been fully parsed, and their attributes can be kept on a parallel value stack next to the grammar symbols. At the reduction the semantic action computes A's attributes from those of X₁…Xₖ (still on the value stack), pops k values and pushes the new one. Evaluation therefore needs no tree and happens in a single left-to-right pass, one action per reduction — so the number of semantic actions executed equals the number of productions used in the rightmost derivation."""),
           ("L-attributed definitions: single left-to-right depth-first evaluation",
            """An SDD is L-attributed if each inherited attribute of a symbol Xᵢ on the right side of A → X₁…Xₙ depends only on the inherited attributes of A and on attributes of X₁…Xᵢ₋₁ (the symbols to the left). A depth-first left-to-right traversal evaluates a node's inherited attributes when it is first visited (all of the data they need are already available: from the parent or from earlier siblings) and its synthesized attributes when it is left. Since the dependency graph consists only of edges pointing from parent to child, from left sibling to right sibling and from children to parent, there are no cycles and this order is a topological order. Every S-attributed definition is L-attributed; L-attributed definitions fit naturally into LL (top-down) parsing."""),
           ("Evaluating E.val on the parse tree of 3 + 4 * 5",
            """With E → E₁ + T {E.val = E₁.val + T.val}, E → T, T → T₁ * F {T.val = T₁.val * F.val}, T → F, F → num {F.val = num.lexval}: the parse tree puts 4 * 5 below the right operand of +, because * is generated lower in the grammar (higher precedence). Bottom-up: F(4) = 4, F(5) = 5, T(4 * 5) = 20, F(3) = 3, T = 3, E = 3 + 20 = 23. The number of reductions is 8: F → num three times, T → F twice (for 3 and 4), T → T * F once, E → T once and E → E + T once. The grammar's structure, not the evaluation order, gives the correct precedence."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Typical SDT applications",
            """Expression evaluation, infix-to-postfix translation (an action printing the operator after the two operands), building syntax trees, type checking (type attributes, coercion), filling the symbol table from declarations (the type of D → T L passed down as an inherited attribute L.in), code generation (attributes holding the code or the temporary name), and computing the length or the number of nodes of a tree. In a translation scheme actions embedded inside a production run at the position they occupy; at the end of a production they run at reduction time. Postfix of a + b * c is a b c * +."""),
           ("Scoping and storage",
            """Static (lexical) scoping binds a non-local name according to the program text; dynamic scoping binds it according to the call chain at run time, so the same code can print different values. A program in which f prints x declared globally while g declares a local x and calls f prints the global value under static scoping and g's local value under dynamic scoping. Block-structured languages use a stack of symbol tables; the nesting depth and the access link or display find non-local variables in nested procedures."""),
       ])

deepen("cd-intermediate-runtime",
       proofs=[
           ("A DAG shares common sub-expressions: counting nodes with value numbering",
            """For the expression (a + b) * (a + b) + (a + b) * c the syntax tree has 6 operator nodes: three copies of a + b (3), two multiplications (2) and the outer addition (1). To build the DAG, give every distinct (operator, operand-numbers) combination one node: a + b gets one node n₁, (n₁ * n₁) gets n₂, (n₁ * c) gets n₃ and (n₂ + n₃) gets n₄ — **4** operator nodes. Value numbering finds common sub-expressions by looking each new expression up in a hash table keyed by (op, number of left, number of right), which is valid as long as no operand has been reassigned in between (kill the entries for a redefined variable)."""),
           ("Parameter passing: call by reference versus call by value with aliasing",
            """Consider f(x, y) { x = x + 1; y = y + x; } called as f(a, a) with a = 2. **Call by value:** x and y are independent copies of 2; x becomes 3 and y becomes 2 + 3 = 5, but a is untouched: a = 2. **Call by reference:** x and y are both aliases of a; x = x + 1 sets a = 3; y = y + x computes a = a + a = 6. **Call by value-result** copies in and copies out at return: the final copy-back order decides whether a becomes 3 or 5. **Call by name** re-evaluates the actual parameter expression at each use (like macro expansion), so with an expression such as a[i] a change to i inside the callee changes which element is used. These differences are the classic source of GATE questions."""),
           ("Activation records and stack depth",
            """Every active procedure call owns an activation record (frame) on the stack holding the return address, saved registers, parameters, local variables and temporaries (plus a control link to the caller's frame and, for nested procedures, an access link). Because frames follow a LIFO discipline the stack grows on call and shrinks on return, which is what makes recursion possible. The maximum stack usage is the maximum, over all moments of the execution, of the total size of the live frames — for linear recursion n·(frame size), for divide and conquer recursion on balanced inputs log n·(frame size). Heap objects outlive calls and need explicit deallocation or garbage collection."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Three-address code representations",
            """**Quadruples** (op, arg1, arg2, result) store the result explicitly and allow instructions to be moved freely; **triples** (op, arg1, arg2) refer to results by instruction number, so moving instructions requires renumbering; **indirect triples** add a list of pointers to triples to allow reordering cheaply. Three-address code for x = a + b * c − d / e needs 5 instructions: t1 = b * c; t2 = a + t1; t3 = d / e; t4 = t2 − t3; x = t4. Boolean expressions translate to jumping code with true/false labels and backpatching to fill in unknown targets in a single pass. Array access a[i] on a 4-byte element array computes the address base + 4·i."""),
           ("Run-time storage organisation",
            """The address space of a process contains the code (text), static data (globals and static variables), the heap (grows upward) and the stack (grows downward). Procedure call sequence: the caller evaluates and pushes arguments, saves the return address and transfers control; the callee allocates its frame, saves registers and initialises locals; the return restores them. Garbage collection and reference counting manage the heap in managed languages. Dangling references arise when a pointer outlives its object (for instance a pointer to a local variable of a returned function)."""),
       ])

deepen("cd-optimization-dataflow",
       proofs=[
           ("Leaders and basic blocks",
            """A basic block is a maximal sequence of consecutive instructions with a single entry (its first instruction) and a single exit (its last instruction), so control can only enter at the top and leave at the bottom. The first instruction of any block must be a **leader**: (1) the first instruction of the program; (2) the target of any jump (otherwise control would enter mid-block); (3) the instruction immediately after a jump (it is reached by fall-through from a branch that might not be taken, so it begins a new entry path). Every block runs from one leader up to (but not including) the next. Counting leaders therefore counts basic blocks: for a 10-instruction program with jumps 2 → 9 and 6 → 2 the leaders are {1, 2, 3, 7, 9}, giving 5 blocks."""),
           ("Live-variable analysis converges: monotone dataflow equations",
            """Define for each block B: IN[B] = use[B] ∪ (OUT[B] − def[B]) and OUT[B] = ⋃ IN[S] over successors S. Start with all sets empty and iterate. Each equation is monotone (growing OUT can only grow IN) and the sets are subsets of the finite set of variables, so each IN/OUT set can only increase and never exceeds the variable set: the iteration reaches a fixed point after a finite number of rounds, which is the *least* solution. Processing blocks in reverse order of a depth-first numbering speeds convergence (a few passes for reducible graphs). The same framework covers forward problems (reaching definitions, available expressions) by reversing the direction and choosing the meet operator: union for 'may' problems and intersection for 'must' problems."""),
           ("Register allocation as graph colouring",
            """Two variables **interfere** if one is live at a point where the other is defined (they cannot share a register). Build the interference graph with a node per variable and an edge per interference. An allocation using k registers is a proper k-colouring of this graph: adjacent nodes must differ. The minimum number of registers needed without spilling is the chromatic number χ of the graph; a triangle forces χ ≥ 3. Chaitin's heuristic repeatedly removes a node with fewer than k neighbours (it can always be coloured later), pushes it on a stack, and when none exists spills a variable to memory; the colours are then assigned in reverse order. Finding the chromatic number is NP-hard in general, but the graphs of straight-line code are interval graphs, for which greedy colouring is optimal."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Data-flow analyses summarised",
            """**Reaching definitions** (forward, ∪): gen = definitions created in the block, kill = other definitions of the same variable; used for constant propagation and detecting uninitialised variables. **Available expressions** (forward, ∩, initialise non-entry blocks to the universal set): an expression is available if it has been computed on every path and its operands not redefined since; used for common sub-expression elimination. **Live variables** (backward, ∪): used for dead-code elimination and register allocation. **Very busy expressions** (backward, ∩): used for code hoisting. In OUT[B] = gen[B] ∪ (IN[B] − kill[B]) the 'must' analyses intersect over predecessors."""),
           ("Optimisations list with examples",
            """Constant folding (3 * 4 → 12), constant and copy propagation, algebraic simplification (x * 1 → x), strength reduction (x * 2 → x << 1), common sub-expression elimination (using a DAG or available expressions), dead-code elimination, loop-invariant code motion (hoist computations that do not depend on the loop), induction-variable elimination, loop unrolling, function inlining, tail-call optimisation and peephole optimisation (look at a small window of generated code). An optimisation must preserve the observable behaviour: be careful with aliasing, volatile variables, floating-point rounding and side effects."""),
       ])
